Ethiopia
Several regions in Ethiopia are still suffering from acute food insecurity, mostly caused by lack of resilience before droughts and climatic patterns. However, Africa’s second most-populous country has made significant progress in fighting hunger in the last two decades, with significant support from FAO on policymaking. With double-digit economic growth in the last 10 years, the Government has put in place public programmes and policies to fight unemployment and improve education or public health that have been paired with indirect subsidies (on wheat, electricity or fuel) and a nascent social security network. Differences across regions are still important, but the whole package of measures has paved the way for the prevalence of undernourishment to decline by 20 percentage points since the year 2000.
