Biodiversity is understood at three levels:
Ecosystem diversity refers to different habitats such as temperate or tropical forests, mountains, cold and hot deserts, oceans, wetlands, rivers and coral reefs. Each ecosystem is characterized by complex relationships between living components such as plants and animals and non-living components such as soil, air and water.
Species diversity
refers to the variety of different species such as honeybees, tuna, wheat and yeast.
